WEBVTT

00:00.600 --> 00:01.773
Module 7.

00:02.123 --> 00:05.048
Individual controls: the daily checklist.

00:05.398 --> 00:08.881
This module turns the course into a short personal routine.

00:09.231 --> 00:12.505
It is the checklist each lawyer and staff member applies

00:12.505 --> 00:15.779
every time AI-assisted work is about to be relied on.

00:17.529 --> 00:21.063
By the end of this module you will be able to:

00:21.063 --> 00:24.596
apply the nine-point personal checklist; know when to escalate to a

00:24.596 --> 00:28.129
supervisor or the Governance Lead; keep your own AI use log.

00:29.879 --> 00:32.596
Here is the nine-point checklist to apply every time

00:32.596 --> 00:35.011
you are about to rely on AI-assisted work.

00:35.361 --> 00:38.925
One: is this tool approved by the firm for this task?

00:39.275 --> 00:43.931
Two: did any client or privileged data go into it, and was that allowed?

00:44.281 --> 00:47.531
Three: was the prompt grounded in the actual sources?

00:47.881 --> 00:52.374
Four: have all five verification checks been done against primary sources?

00:52.724 --> 00:55.720
Five: is the law current and the jurisdiction right?

00:56.070 --> 01:01.608
Six: have I applied my own professional judgment, rather than simply adopting the output?

01:01.958 --> 01:05.708
Seven: has the right review tier been completed and recorded?

01:06.058 --> 01:09.036
Eight: is AI use disclosed wherever a court,

01:09.036 --> 01:12.014
a client or an engagement letter requires it?

01:12.364 --> 01:15.591
And nine: am I billing actual time only?

01:15.941 --> 01:18.960
If the answer to any of these is no, or I'm

01:18.960 --> 01:21.978
not sure, stop and fix it before the work goes out.

01:23.728 --> 01:25.121
Know when to escalate.

01:25.471 --> 01:28.807
Go to your supervising lawyer when you can't verify a

01:28.807 --> 01:32.143
source, when the AI's answer conflicts with your own understanding,

01:32.143 --> 01:35.479
or when a deadline means the review would be rushed.

01:35.829 --> 01:39.513
Go to the firm's AI Governance Lead when you want to use

01:39.513 --> 01:43.197
a tool that isn't approved, when red-category data is involved, or when

01:43.197 --> 01:46.267
you suspect a tool is producing a pattern of errors.

01:46.617 --> 01:50.020
And escalate immediately if you discover an error in work

01:50.020 --> 01:53.083
that has already been sent, filed or relied on.

01:53.433 --> 01:56.231
Module nine covers exactly what to do.

01:57.981 --> 02:00.953
Finally, keep a simple personal AI log, even

02:00.953 --> 02:03.554
if your firm's systems also log usage.

02:03.904 --> 02:07.304
Note the date, the tool and plan, the matter number, and

02:07.304 --> 02:10.394
what was checked, with a reference to the review record.

02:10.744 --> 02:13.943
It takes seconds, and it means you can always answer the

02:13.943 --> 02:16.851
question: what did you use, and what did you check?

02:18.601 --> 02:20.203
Let's recap the key points.

02:20.553 --> 02:23.920
Nine questions, every time, before reliance.

02:24.270 --> 02:29.390
Escalate early: supervisor for doubts, Governance Lead for tools and data.

02:29.740 --> 02:32.967
Errors already relied on are escalated immediately.

02:34.717 --> 02:35.925
Knowledge check.

02:36.275 --> 02:38.423
You can't find a case the AI cited,

02:38.423 --> 02:40.570
and the brief is due in one hour.

02:40.920 --> 02:41.803
What do you do?

02:42.153 --> 02:45.578
Option A: File it; the AI is usually right.

02:45.928 --> 02:51.350
Option B: Remove or replace the citation and escalate to your supervising lawyer.

02:51.700 --> 02:55.055
Option C: Ask the AI to confirm the case.

02:55.405 --> 02:58.098
Take a moment to decide before the answer appears.

03:03.848 --> 03:05.462
The answer is option B.

03:05.812 --> 03:08.819
An unverifiable authority never goes into a filing.

03:09.169 --> 03:12.733
Remove or replace it, and escalate the time pressure.
